{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "log-02-basic-evaluation",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Basic Logarithm Values and Inverse Powers",
    "description": "Evaluate logarithms exactly by identifying the exponent that a valid base must take to produce the argument.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop definitions and laws step by step, explain why each transformation is valid, show intermediate calculations and checks, and use targeted practice with changed-value remedial retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 10 “Logarithms,” printed pages 297–307. Approved scope: Use the defining equivalence log_a(a^r)=r and a^(log_a x)=x under valid domains. Evaluate exact values by rewriting arguments as powers of the base, including unit arguments, base arguments, reciprocals, fractional exponents, and bases below 1. Teach the checks log_a(1)=0 and log_a(a)=1 only for allowed bases, and use inverse checks to catch sign mistakes. Do not use logarithm tables or change of base as the main method in this subtopic."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Welcome to Basic Logarithm Values and Inverse Powers. Evaluate logarithms exactly by identifying the exponent that a valid base must take to produce the argument.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_1": {
      "id": "lesson_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Once log_a(x)=y is read as a^y=x, evaluation becomes an exponent-recognition task. For integer powers, factor the argument until it is visibly a power of the base. For noninteger powers, keep the exponent exact rather than approximating.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_2": {
      "id": "lesson_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The two inverse identities are log_a(a^r)=r and a^(log_a x)=x. In the first, the logarithm undoes a power; in the second, the exponential undoes a logarithm. The domains still apply: a>0, a≠1, x>0.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_3": {
      "id": "lesson_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The unit argument gives log_a(1)=0 because a^0=1 for every allowed base. The base argument gives log_a(a)=1 because a^1=a. These are useful anchors, but they do not authorize a base of 1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_4": {
      "id": "lesson_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the argument is a reciprocal power, preserve the negative exponent. Since 1/a^n=a^(−n), log_a(1/a^n)=−n. For example log_7(1/343)=−3 because 343=7^3.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_5": {
      "id": "lesson_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A fractional exponent may be the answer. Since 25^(1/2)=5, log_25(5)=1/2. Since 8^(2/3)=4, log_8(4)=2/3. Check by raising the base to the proposed answer.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_6": {
      "id": "lesson_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the base is a reciprocal, rewrite it as a power of a familiar base. For log_(1/4)(8), solve (1/4)^y=8. Since 2^(−2y)=2^3, y=−3/2. Rewriting exposes the sign and fraction clearly.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_7": {
      "id": "lesson_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a base between 0 and 1, the exponent-to-value direction reverses. As y increases, (1/2)^y decreases. Therefore an argument greater than 1 requires a negative logarithm value, while an argument between 0 and 1 requires a positive value.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_8": {
      "id": "lesson_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "An expression like a^(log_a x) can be simplified to x only when a is a valid base and x>0. For example, 5^(log_5 12)=12. Do not change the base in one part of the expression while keeping the log base in another.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_9": {
      "id": "lesson_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: evaluate 16^(log_4 3). Since 16=4^2, write (4^2)^(log_4 3)=4^(2 log_4 3)=(4^(log_4 3))^2=3^2=9. The power rule can convert a complicated inverse expression into a familiar value.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_10": {
      "id": "lesson_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: evaluate log_27(1/9). Write 27=3^3 and 1/9=3^(−2). Then 3^(3y)=3^(−2), so y=−2/3. Verify 27^(−2/3)=1/9.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_11": {
      "id": "lesson_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When no simple power is apparent, an exact logarithm may be the best answer. For example log_2(7) is exact even though it is not an integer; write an approximation only if the question supplies or requests one.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_12": {
      "id": "lesson_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use sign and magnitude as a check. With base greater than 1, an argument above 1 gives a positive log and an argument between 0 and 1 gives a negative log. With a base between 0 and 1, those signs reverse.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_13": {
      "id": "lesson_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Do not treat a logarithm as an ordinary fraction. log_a(x) is not x/a. It is the exponent satisfying a^y=x; a fraction appears only after a valid identity such as change of base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_14": {
      "id": "lesson_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The common-log shorthand may hide a base: in this chapter log(x) without a written base means log_10(x). Do not assume this shorthand for a logarithm that visibly carries another base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_15": {
      "id": "lesson_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Finish every evaluation by substituting the proposed result into the defining power equation. If the base raised to the answer does not equal the argument, the answer cannot be correct.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_16"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_16": {
      "id": "lesson_16",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Fractional powers are useful when the argument and base share a root relationship. To evaluate log_16(8), let the answer be y: (2^4)^y=2^3, so 4y=3 and y=3/4. Verify by raising 16 to three-fourths.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_17"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_17": {
      "id": "lesson_17",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A negative exponent in the answer is tested the same way as a positive exponent. For log_5(1/25), the candidate −2 works because 5^(−2)=1/25. The negative sign belongs to the exponent, while the argument stays positive.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_18"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_18": {
      "id": "lesson_18",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When both numbers are powers of a common positive number, equate those exponents. If log_8(4)=y, rewrite 8=2^3 and 4=2^2; then 2^(3y)=2^2, so y=2/3. This method avoids memorizing special logarithm values.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_19"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_19": {
      "id": "lesson_19",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The inverse identity has a domain attached: a^(log_a x)=x requires x>0. It cannot be used to turn a^(log_a 0) into zero, because log_a(0) does not exist as a real number.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Checkpoint 1: Basic Logarithm Values and Inverse Powers. The answer need not be an integer; test a fractional exponent. Read the full prompt, identify the relevant definition or law, and check the domain before choosing.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
